entry.summary:
Owners often ask why a search engine or an AI assistant describes their business strangely, or not at all. One common, boring cause: the words on the homepage only exist after JavaScript runs. Some crawlers render pages fully. Others read the raw HTML and move on. If your headline, your services and your city are not in that raw HTML, part of the web may never see them. Here is a quick way to compare what a person sees with what a simple crawler sees, in about thirty lines of Python. import re, html, requests
def visible_text(markup: str) -> str: markup = re.sub(r"<(script|style|noscript)[^>]*>.*?</\1>", " ", markup, flags=re.S | re.I) text = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", markup) return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", html.unescape(text)).strip()
